          type: "imageText",
          title: "From Prediction to Management",
          content: "<p>For decades the field labored under an impossible expectation: that clinicians could reliably <em>predict</em> who would become violent. The research literature has thoroughly dismantled that premise. Violence is a low base-rate event, and even the best actuarial tools produce substantial false positives. The modern paradigm therefore reframes the task from prediction to <strong>risk management</strong>. We do not pretend to forecast a binary future; we estimate the likelihood, imminence, severity, and modifiability of harm under specified conditions, and then we intervene to lower it. This shift matters enormously for both clinical practice and legal defensibility, because the standard of care is not perfect prediction but reasonable professional judgment applied through a recognizable process. Keep this reframing in mind as we proceed: your obligation is to assess and manage thoughtfully, document honestly, and act reasonably, not to achieve clairvoyance.</p>",
